1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,730
Ask three friends to list the
good students in your class.

2
00:00:03,730 --> 00:00:05,762
You will get three different lists.

3
00:00:06,162 --> 00:00:10,318
Now ask them to list the students
who ride a bicycle to college.

4
00:00:10,318 --> 00:00:12,480
All three lists match, exactly.

5
00:00:12,930 --> 00:00:15,827
Aarav noticed this in the
college corridor today.

6
00:00:16,177 --> 00:00:17,791
Same class, same friends.

7
00:00:17,791 --> 00:00:20,687
Why does one list work,
and the other one fail?

8
00:00:21,187 --> 00:00:25,145
The answer is the very first
idea of Class 11 maths.

9
00:00:25,145 --> 00:00:26,836
It is called a set.

10
00:00:27,486 --> 00:00:28,764
So what is a set?

11
00:00:29,114 --> 00:00:32,339
A set is a well defined
collection of objects.

12
00:00:32,739 --> 00:00:35,586
Well defined means it
passes one simple test.

13
00:00:35,586 --> 00:00:40,020
For any object, everyone can say
clearly: is it in, or is it out?

14
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:41,817
Watch the test run.

15
00:00:41,817 --> 00:00:44,562
Our rule is: even numbers less than ten.

16
00:00:44,962 --> 00:00:45,862
One?

17
00:00:45,451 --> 00:00:47,340
It is odd, so it is out.

18
00:00:47,340 --> 00:00:48,240
Two?

19
00:00:47,829 --> 00:00:49,602
Even, and less than ten.

20
00:00:49,602 --> 00:00:50,502
It is in.

21
00:00:50,846 --> 00:00:52,964
Every number gets a clear answer.

22
00:00:52,964 --> 00:00:56,047
So the collection is
two, four, six and eight.

23
00:00:56,047 --> 00:00:57,162
That is a set.

24
00:00:57,812 --> 00:00:58,904
Side by side.

25
00:00:58,904 --> 00:01:00,875
Students who ride a bicycle.

26
00:01:00,875 --> 00:01:03,169
Kiran rides one, so she is in.

27
00:01:03,169 --> 00:01:05,150
Joel walks, so he is out.

28
00:01:05,150 --> 00:01:06,333
Everyone agrees.

29
00:01:06,733 --> 00:01:07,719
Good students.

30
00:01:07,719 --> 00:01:08,760
Is Meera good?

31
00:01:08,760 --> 00:01:11,175
One friend says yes, another says no.

32
00:01:11,175 --> 00:01:14,180
There is no clear answer,
so it is not a set.

33
00:01:14,779 --> 00:01:15,745
Now you try.

34
00:01:15,745 --> 00:01:19,664
Is the collection of months that
begin with the letter J a set?

35
00:01:19,664 --> 00:01:20,864
Pause and decide.

36
00:01:24,064 --> 00:01:24,964
Yes!

37
00:01:24,609 --> 00:01:26,469
January, June and July.

38
00:01:26,469 --> 00:01:30,371
Anyone who checks a calendar
gets the same three months.

39
00:01:31,021 --> 00:01:34,883
The objects inside a set are
called its elements, or members.

40
00:01:35,233 --> 00:01:38,077
We name a set with a
capital letter, like A.

41
00:01:38,077 --> 00:01:40,577
Its elements are small letters or numbers.

42
00:01:40,977 --> 00:01:44,948
If two is in A, we write two,
then this curly symbol, then A.

43
00:01:44,948 --> 00:01:46,791
It reads: two belongs to A.

44
00:01:47,541 --> 00:01:50,830
If three is not in A, we
strike the symbol through.

45
00:01:50,830 --> 00:01:52,641
Three does not belong to A.

46
00:01:53,391 --> 00:01:54,398
Remember this.

47
00:01:54,398 --> 00:01:58,728
A set is a collection with a clear
in or out answer for every object.

48
00:01:59,428 --> 00:02:02,909
Some sets are used so often
that they get their own letters.

49
00:02:03,259 --> 00:02:04,920
N is the natural numbers.

50
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:07,236
One, two, three, and on forever.

51
00:02:07,636 --> 00:02:09,130
Z is the integers.

52
00:02:09,130 --> 00:02:11,891
It adds zero and the negative numbers.

53
00:02:12,291 --> 00:02:14,394
Q is the rational numbers.

54
00:02:14,394 --> 00:02:19,749
Every number p over q, where p and
q are integers and q is not zero.

55
00:02:20,149 --> 00:02:21,781
R is the real numbers.

56
00:02:21,781 --> 00:02:24,324
Every single point on the number line.

57
00:02:24,974 --> 00:02:25,874
Quick one.

58
00:02:25,862 --> 00:02:27,699
Is zero a natural number?

59
00:02:27,699 --> 00:02:28,955
Pause and think.

60
00:02:32,155 --> 00:02:33,055
No!

61
00:02:32,646 --> 00:02:34,782
Natural numbers start at one.

62
00:02:34,782 --> 00:02:37,632
Zero first appears in Z, the integers.

63
00:02:38,282 --> 00:02:40,325
Now, how do we write a set down?

64
00:02:40,325 --> 00:02:41,536
There are two ways.

65
00:02:41,886 --> 00:02:43,760
The first is roster form.

66
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:48,236
List every element inside curly
brackets, with commas between them.

67
00:02:48,636 --> 00:02:52,566
Our set becomes two, four, six,
eight, inside curly brackets.

68
00:02:53,166 --> 00:02:55,105
The second is set builder form.

69
00:02:55,105 --> 00:02:59,075
Instead of listing the elements,
we write the rule they all follow.

70
00:02:59,475 --> 00:03:00,823
Read it like this.

71
00:03:00,823 --> 00:03:05,449
The set of all x, such that x is an
even natural number, and x is less

72
00:03:05,449 --> 00:03:06,349
than ten.

73
00:03:06,995 --> 00:03:09,179
The curly bracket means the set of.

74
00:03:09,179 --> 00:03:10,804
x stands for any element.

75
00:03:10,804 --> 00:03:12,474
The colon means such that.

76
00:03:12,474 --> 00:03:14,424
After the colon comes the rule.

77
00:03:15,174 --> 00:03:17,201
Now watch the rule do the work.

78
00:03:17,201 --> 00:03:19,457
Change the limit from ten to twenty.

79
00:03:19,857 --> 00:03:22,897
The rule stays short, but
the list keeps growing.

80
00:03:22,897 --> 00:03:25,810
That is why set builder
form wins for big sets.

81
00:03:26,460 --> 00:03:27,612
Side by side.

82
00:03:27,612 --> 00:03:30,183
Roster form shows you every element.

83
00:03:30,183 --> 00:03:32,221
It is best for small sets.

84
00:03:32,621 --> 00:03:35,029
Set builder form shows you the rule.

85
00:03:35,029 --> 00:03:39,455
It is best for large or endless sets,
like all the real numbers between

86
00:03:39,455 --> 00:03:40,508
zero and one.

87
00:03:41,108 --> 00:03:42,844
Now, one worked example.

88
00:03:43,194 --> 00:03:48,028
A is the set of all x, such that x
is a prime number less than twenty.

89
00:03:48,028 --> 00:03:49,715
Write A in roster form.

90
00:03:49,715 --> 00:03:50,847
Is nine in A?

91
00:03:51,347 --> 00:03:52,247
Step one.

92
00:03:52,205 --> 00:03:56,076
A prime number has exactly
two factors, one and itself.

93
00:03:56,826 --> 00:03:57,726
Step two.

94
00:03:57,644 --> 00:04:00,256
Test every number from two to nineteen.

95
00:04:01,006 --> 00:04:01,908
Step three.

96
00:04:01,908 --> 00:04:06,676
The primes are two, three, five,
seven, eleven, thirteen, seventeen and

97
00:04:06,676 --> 00:04:07,576
nineteen.

98
00:04:08,171 --> 00:04:09,071
Step four.

99
00:04:09,046 --> 00:04:10,907
Nine is three times three.

100
00:04:10,907 --> 00:04:14,487
It has three factors, so
nine does not belong to A.

101
00:04:15,237 --> 00:04:18,537
So A has eight elements, and
two is the only even one.

102
00:04:19,187 --> 00:04:20,652
Now, the exam trap.

103
00:04:21,002 --> 00:04:24,993
Write the set of integers x such
that x squared is less than ten.

104
00:04:24,993 --> 00:04:27,394
Many students write one, two, three.

105
00:04:27,794 --> 00:04:30,522
But integers include
zero and the negatives.

106
00:04:30,522 --> 00:04:34,191
Minus three squared is nine,
which is also less than ten.

107
00:04:34,191 --> 00:04:37,955
The full set has seven elements,
from minus three to three.

108
00:04:38,605 --> 00:04:40,057
Where is this asked?

109
00:04:40,057 --> 00:04:44,698
In JEE Main, as questions that give
a set in set builder form and ask how

110
00:04:44,698 --> 00:04:46,199
many elements it has.

111
00:04:46,799 --> 00:04:47,948
Exam level now.

112
00:04:47,948 --> 00:04:50,256
How many elements are in this set?

113
00:04:50,256 --> 00:04:51,454
Pause and solve.

114
00:04:56,854 --> 00:04:58,217
The answer is C.

115
00:04:58,217 --> 00:04:59,243
Twenty five.

116
00:04:59,743 --> 00:05:02,910
If n is even, n squared minus one is odd.

117
00:05:02,910 --> 00:05:05,899
An odd number is never divisible by eight.

118
00:05:06,399 --> 00:05:11,614
If n is odd, n minus one and n plus
one are two even numbers in a row.

119
00:05:11,614 --> 00:05:14,185
One of them is a multiple of four.

120
00:05:14,685 --> 00:05:18,937
So their product is a multiple of
two times four, which is eight.

121
00:05:18,937 --> 00:05:23,791
Every odd n from one to forty nine works,
even one, because zero is divisible

122
00:05:23,791 --> 00:05:24,691
by eight.

123
00:05:24,587 --> 00:05:26,480
That is twenty five numbers.

124
00:05:27,080 --> 00:05:28,544
Here is your recap.

125
00:05:28,544 --> 00:05:30,049
Screenshot this card.

126
00:05:33,249 --> 00:05:36,085
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

127
00:05:36,485 --> 00:05:37,385
One.

128
00:05:36,965 --> 00:05:39,051
Why is good students not a set?

129
00:05:39,951 --> 00:05:40,851
Two.

130
00:05:40,471 --> 00:05:43,405
Write the vowels of
English in roster form.

131
00:05:44,305 --> 00:05:45,205
Three.

132
00:05:44,876 --> 00:05:47,720
What does the colon mean
in set builder form?

133
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:51,400
Next time: the word SCHOOL has two O's.

134
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:53,696
So why does its set have only one?

135
00:05:53,696 --> 00:05:56,521
We find out in roster
and set builder forms.
